From 5904466eaa5fa96715e1657734c4e8275fe77854 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 01:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(deepseek-v4.1): read disk Engram rows beside the forward
 launch

With disk-resident Engram tables the host read both Engram layers' rows
before launching the forward: it waited for the hash IDs, read the rows
with io_uring, decoded them, and only then launched the graph, so the GPU
idled through both reads and the graph launch (about 1.85 ms of a 40-52 ms
single-stream step on three DGX Sparks).

SPARK3_ENGRAM_ASYNC=1 splits the preparation. The main thread hashes,
resets a per-layer ready flag, copies the row IDs to the host, and queues
the row decode on a side stream behind a host gate (cuStreamWaitValue32 on a
mapped word), then returns so the forward launches at once. A reader thread
does host work only: it waits for the IDs, reads the rows, and releases the
gate, also on failure. Each Engram layer waits on its flag, captured into
the CUDA graph, before reading the staged rows. All kernel launches and
stream operations stay on the main thread. The reader's ID wait and the next
step's join have deadlines, so a stalled device fails the step instead of
hanging.

Off by default. Disk tables always carry the flag and the forward always
waits on it, so traced and captured graphs are identical with the path on
or off (the compile cache does not key on the environment); with it off the
flag stays 1. The rows and their decode are unchanged, only their timing.
---
 vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/common/engram.py | 248 +++++++++++++++++++++
 vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/nvidia/model.py  |  52 +++++
 2 files changed, 300 insertions(+)

